on-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cheng</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Ma</surname><given-names>JZ</given-names></name><name><surname>Wright</surname><given-names>FA</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Gao</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Nonparametric disequilibrium mapping of functional sites using haplotypes of multiple tightly linked single-nucleotide polymorphism markers</article-title><source>Genetics</source><volume>164</volume><fpage>1175</fpage><lpage>1187</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12871923</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030046-b053"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Chakravarti</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Cutler</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Exhaustive allelic transmission disequilibrium tests as a new approach to genome-wide association studies</article-title><source>Nat Genet</source><volume>36</volume><fpage>1181</fpage><lpage>1188</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15502828</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030046-b054"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mathias</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Gao</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Goldstein</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>Wilson</surname><given-names>AF</given-names></name><name><surname>Pugh</surname><given-names>EW</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>A graphical assessment of <italic>p</italic>-values from sliding window haplotype tests of association to identify asthma susceptibility loci on chromosome 11q</article-title><source>BMC Genet</source><volume>7</volume><fpage>38</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16774684</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030046-b055"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>CM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Genetic association studies: Design, analysis and interpretation</article-title><source>Brief Bioinform</source><volume>3</volume><fpage>146</fpage><lpage>153</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12139434</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030046-b056"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Everitt</surname><given-names>BS</given-names></name></person-group><year>1993</year><source>Cluster analysis, 3rd edition</source><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Arnold</publisher-name><fpage>55</fpage><lpage>89</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig id="pgen-0030046-g001" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><title>Examples of Distributions of Clusters at the Best Partition (T&#x0005b;best&#x0005d;) in 2,000 Simulations with the Two Clustering Methods under Different Settings</title><p>A-1 and A-2 present the distributions of clusters at T&#x0005b;best&#x0005d; using our weighted cladistic analysis method and CLADHC, respectively, when original haplotypes have a high diversity (the number of distinct haplotypes is 12); B-1 and B-2 display the distributions of clusters at T&#x0005b;best&#x0005d; corresponding to using our proposed method and CLADHC when original haplotypes have a low diversity (the number of distinct haplotypes is eight). Other parameters considered in the simulations are the same for A-1, A-2, B-1, and B-2, which include: <italic>q</italic> &#x0003d; 0.1, <italic>r</italic> &#x0003d; 1.75, and assuming an additive model.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pgen.0030046.g001"/></fig><table-wrap id="pgen-0030046-t001" content-type="1col" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Type-I Error (&#x00025;) of the Global Test (Accounting for Multiple Testing) Based on the LLR Statistic under a Logistic Regression Model at 5&#x00025; Significance Level and Various Conditions</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pgen.0030046.t001"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pgen-0030046-t002" content-type="1col" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Powers of the Association Tests Based on the LLR Statistic under a Logistic Regression Model at 5&#x00025; Significance Level</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pgen.0030046.t002"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pgen-0030046-t003" content-type="1col" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>The Best Partitions of Haplotypes and Their Corresponding Odds Ratios for RA Disease</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pgen.0030046.t003"/></table-wrap></sec><fn-group><fn id="ack3" fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing interests.</bold> The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn><fn id="ack1" fn-type="con"><p><bold>Author contributions.</bold> HWD conceived and designed the experiments. JL performed the experiments. JL and CP analyzed the data. JL, CP, and HWD contributed to writing the paper.</p></fn><fn id="ack2" fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding.</bold> The investigators of this work were partially supported by grants from the National Institutes of Health (R01 AR050496, R01 GM 60402, and K01 AR 02170). The study also benefited from grant support from the National Science Foundation of China, Huo Ying Dong Education Foundation, HuNan Province, Xi'an Jiaotong University, and the Ministry of Education China.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>